---

<section
  id="capabilities"
  class="py-12 sm:py-16 px-4 sm:px-6 max-w-6xl mx-auto border-t border-ink/15"
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">
      What OmniBuf does beyond Protobuf
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="border-t border-ink/15 divide-y divide-ink/12">
    <!-- Row 1: Schema-Less Wire Inspection -->
    <div
      id="explicit-wire-types"
      class="scroll-mt-16 py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-start"
    >
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 space-y-2.5 min-w-0">
        <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-snug text-ink">
          Self-describing wire types
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]">
          Protobuf only has a 3-bit wire type, so strings, byte slices, sub-messages, maps, and
          packed arrays all get lumped into <code>Wire Type 2</code>. Fixed 32-bit and 64-bit
          numbers mix floats with signed and unsigned integers. OmniBuf widens the wire type to 4
          bits and puts element types on arrays and maps. The{' '}
          <a href="/tooling/cli" class="doc-link">
            <code>omnibuf</code> CLI
          </a>{' '}
          and <code>DecodeBlind</code> return the exact numbers, booleans, strings, arrays, and maps
          straight from a raw buffer. You only need the schema for things that aren't sent on the
          wire, like enum names, default values, or <code>oneof</code> groupings.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 space-y-3 font-mono text-sm min-w-0">
        <div class="p-3.5 bg-[#FAF8F2] border border-ink/15 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto">
          <!-- prettier-ignore -->
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs font-semibold text-ink/70 mb-1.5">
            <a href="https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protoscope" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="doc-link">$ protoscope packet.pb</a>
            <span class="font-normal">Protobuf</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink/85 leading-snug"><span class="text-[#C23000] font-semibold">3:</span> 1                <span class="text-ink/60"># int or bool?</span>
<span class="text-[#C23000] font-semibold">9:</span> &#123;<span class="text-ink/60">"\x1a\x03FRA\x20*"</span>&#125; <span class="text-ink/60"># string, bytes, or sub-msg?</span></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3.5 bg-ink text-paper border border-ink/15 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs font-semibold text-acid mb-1.5">
            <span>$ omnibuf -c packet.obuf</span>
            <span class="font-normal text-paper/60">OmniBuf</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-paper/95 leading-snug">&#123;<span class="text-acid">"field_3":</span> <span class="text-[#FF7A4D] font-semibold">true</span>, <span class="text-acid">"field_9":</span> &#123;<span class="text-acid">"field_3":</span> <span class="text-paper font-semibold">"FRA"</span>, <span class="text-acid">"field_4":</span> 42&#125;&#125;</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3.5 bg-ink text-paper border border-ink/15 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs font-semibold text-acid mb-1.5">
            <span>$ omnibuf -c packet-field0.obuf</span>
            <span class="font-normal text-paper/60">OmniBuf + Field #0</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-paper/95 leading-snug">&#123;<span class="text-acid">"active":</span> <span class="text-[#FF7A4D] font-semibold">true</span>, <span class="text-acid">"node":</span> &#123;<span class="text-acid">"city":</span> <span class="text-paper font-semibold">"FRA"</span>, <span class="text-acid">"asn":</span> 42&#125;&#125;</pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Row 2: Optional Field #0 Inline Field Names -->
    <div
      id="field-0-names"
      class="scroll-mt-16 py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-start"
    >
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 space-y-3 min-w-0">
        <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-snug text-ink">
          Optional field names in Field #0
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]">
          By default, OmniBuf only writes numeric field IDs to keep packets small. Optional{' '}
          <code>Field #0</code> (<code>omnibuf.v1.SchemaMeta</code>) writes a field-number-to-name
          map once at the start of the packet so the wire bytes carry their own string field names.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[65ch]">
          For arrays and maps of structs, child field names are stored once in{' '}
          <a href="/spec#sec-8-1" class="doc-link">
            <code>SchemaMeta.children</code>
          </a>{' '}
          and inherited by every item in the list. In JSON or MessagePack, an array of 1,000 objects
          repeats the key strings <code>"router_id"</code> and <code>"latency_ns"</code> 1,000
          times. OmniBuf writes those strings once in Field #0 and uses compact field numbers inside
          the elements. Even on our benchmark packet with just 4 <code>Neighbor</code> items, a
          self-describing OmniBuf packet (630 B) is smaller than MessagePack (656 B) and JSON (803
          B).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-6 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm min-w-0">
        <div class="os-window overflow-hidden">
          <div class="os-titlebar">
            <span class="font-mono font-semibold text-ink/80 text-xs">
              1,000-item array: repeated keys vs. Field #0 inheritance
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-ink text-paper p-4 space-y-4">
            <div>
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs mb-2">
                <span class="font-semibold text-[#FF7A4D]">JSON &amp; MessagePack</span>
                <span class="text-paper/60">keys repeated 1,000x</span>
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>"neighbors": [
  &#123;<span class="text-[#FF7A4D]">"router_id"</span>: "fra-1", <span class="text-[#FF7A4D]">"latency_ns"</span>: 420&#125;,
  &#123;<span class="text-[#FF7A4D]">"router_id"</span>: "ams-2", <span class="text-[#FF7A4D]">"latency_ns"</span>: 890&#125;,
  ...
]</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="pt-3.5 border-t border-paper/12 space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 text-xs">
                <span class="font-semibold text-acid">OmniBuf + Field #0</span>
                <span class="text-paper/60">names written once</span>
              </div>
              <pre class="leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code><span class="text-paper/55">// Field #0: name table (once per packet)</span>
<span class="text-acid">10</span>   = "neighbors"
<span class="text-acid">10.1</span> = "router_id"
<span class="text-acid">10.2</span> = "latency_ns"

<span class="text-paper/55">// Field #10: 1,000 items (numeric IDs only)</span>
&#123;<span class="text-acid">1</span>: "fra-1", <span class="text-acid">2</span>: 420&#125;
&#123;<span class="text-acid">1</span>: "ams-2", <span class="text-acid">2</span>: 890&#125;
...</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
